- \`inspected\` — you opened the actual file or a render and made a qualitative observation (silhouette, seam, palette, style match); describe what you saw and give no numbers.
- \`specified\` — a target or requirement from the brief or your own spec; no file was checked. A budget you write into a spec is \`specified\`.
- \`unverified\` — a result that should have been checked but could not be (no file access, no tool, unreadable file), or a value someone else stated that you did not check; say why and name the source.
Never claim a polycount, texel density, loudness, color space, or visual-quality result without \`measured\` or \`inspected\` evidence. Numeric results (polycount, texel density, resolution, loudness, true peak, sample rate, bit depth) and color-space slot checks require \`measured\`; \`inspected\` covers only visual or audible judgments. A budget or target you write into the spec is a requirement and is labelled \`specified\`, not a result. A checklist item is \`pass\` or \`fail\` only with \`measured\` or \`inspected\` evidence. Otherwise record it as \`not checked\` and keep its label (\`specified\` or \`unverified\`).

